#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065250} :activity/id #uuid "073f76c5-241e-56ac-b230-c4ae3778c41c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049362 :topic/id #uuid "eefa1995-be29-5c63-a8ec-6c5199131e6e" :topic/math-academy-id 337} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065365 :tutorial/content "The average rate of change of the function $y=f(x)$ on the interval $[a,a+h]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\dfrac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{h}\n$$\n\nThis can be interpreted as the slope of the secant line that intersects the curve at the points $\\color{blue}P$ and ${\\color{red}Q}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/32/3264f0792cee452453b024a61fb6f58a45290e133d3f84ec1ecf1c14501b4423.png)\n\nThe **instantaneous rate of change** of the function $f(x)$ at the point $x = a$ is the limit of the average rate of change as $h \\to 0$. It is denoted by $f'(a)$ and given by\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac {f(a+h) - f(a)} {h}\n$$\n\nGeometrically, as $h \\to 0$, the point $\\color{red}Q$ moves along the curve $y=f(x)$ towards the point ${\\color{blue}P}$, and the secant transforms into the line that touches the curve at exactly one point $({\\color{blue}P})$. That line is called the **tangent line** to the curve $y=f(x)$ at the point $\\color{blue}P$.\n\n![](images/37/3704b7e4f7a7aec6245d3494ef1e59a2b863e74a50bf5c9ecf471eec3789e7e9.png)\n\nThe value of $f'(a)$ is equal to the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $x=a$." :tutorial/id #uuid "1f6bb245-1f46-5ade-84d4-6fd172b4b227" :tutorial/math-academy-id 322 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "35e9e9bc-7d8d-5422-a6d3-4890eb3c5548" :step/math-academy-id 595 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065251}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064337 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063933 :question/id #uuid "7f0eeea0-e493-5287-9633-8128758d5d57" :question/math-academy-id "e-125"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f2e684-6cd8-5ede-9f70-1a24ada54cb9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049927}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235414}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Instantaneous Rate of Change of a Function at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "28874baa-b4bb-54af-b29b-a7cbfc639bc9" :step/math-academy-id 596 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065252}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065366 :tutorial/content "Another way of defining $f'(a)$ is\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{x\\to a}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(a)} {x-a}\n$$\n\nWe arrive at this definition in the same way as before. But this time, we consider the interval $[a,x]$ instead of $[a,a+h]$, and then take the limit of the average rate of change as $x\\to a$.\n\n![](images/b4/b49c6bba1cb82227e7f47b2d1b91eae85ece94fd5251b79173842179cf7946e0.png)\n\nSometimes, this version is a bit easier to work with, so let's see an example of how to apply it. We should always arrive at the same answer regardless of which definition we use." :tutorial/id #uuid "6731f995-584b-5424-a320-c41c218307f9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 324 :tutorial/title "An Equivalent Definition of Instantaneous Rate of Change"} :step/id #uuid "2fde7718-a29e-5055-884f-3f58ca08aaec" :step/math-academy-id 599 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065253}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065253 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064338 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063934 :question/id #uuid "95edcdc3-a675-5963-9e1b-e9eb0595327d" :question/math-academy-id "e-126"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ce0d18d-f1ee-5654-984d-f191abc9a410" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233067}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point Using the Equivalent Definition"} :step/id #uuid "af0ad9db-1ba6-5216-8e5f-5d768427a09e" :step/math-academy-id 600}] :activity/title "The Instantaneous Rate of Change of a Function at a Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064428}]}
